Chapter: CapAudio

 Sound is its own authority, and asking for it does not buy
 the machine.

 This opening declares [Audio] and nothing else of that family, then reads
 its own capability word back out of the process table. The bits it
 reports are the granted ones -- process-get-cap is what the kernel itself
 tests before it services a syscall -- not a restatement of the row above.

   audio=1    the row was honoured; a program that declares Audio can play.
   device=0   asking to make a sound did not buy the Device family.
   block=0    and it did not buy the disk controller either.

 The zeroes are the test. audio=1 alone would pass just as well on the old
 arrangement, where HdaAudio published [Device.Mmio] and a program that
 wanted to beep declared the Device family to get it: it would have earned
 the audio bit if one had existed, and the device and block bits with it.
 An attenuation is only visible in what it DENIES, so a pin that reports
 only what was granted cannot see this change at all.

 Microphone is reported too, and it must be 0. Speaking and listening are
 separate authorities over one controller, and the easy mistake -- giving
 the whole codec one bit -- would make the right to beep the right to hear
 the room. That is the more dangerous half, and nothing in the granted
 word would say so.

 Bit numbers are the boot table's (cap-* in X86_64Boot.codex): device 16,
 microphone 20, audio 25, block-device 10.

Section: Bits

  cap-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bit (word) (n) = if bit-and word (bit-shl 1 n) /= 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Audio] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("audio "      & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 25))
    print-line-uni ("device "     & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 16))
    print-line-uni ("block "      & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 10))
    print-line-uni ("microphone " & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 20))
  end
